      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered the order. The FDA label doesn't support that dose.\n\nThe current US label for metformin immediate-release (Glucophage) sets a maximum of 2,550 mg/day. Doses are given in divided amounts, and the largest labeled single dose is 850 mg three times daily. The extended-release maximum is 2,000 mg/day. An order of 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day, which is twice the labeled maximum. If you're looking at a different source, please check it. It may list a total daily dose, or it may be a different product.\n\nA dose this far above the label carries a real risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity. I'm not willing to enter it, even with confirmation. If a clinical reason exists for going above the label, such as a specific protocol or a toxicology or endocrine recommendation, it should go through pharmacy and the attending as a documented override.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum)\n- Metformin 1700 mg/day total, for example 850 mg PO twice daily with meals\n- Another dose and frequency within the labeled range\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
